%U A400399 72,73,75,76,78,79,81,82,84,85,87,88,89,91,92,94,95,97,98
%N A400399 Value of k that maximizes P(X < Y), where X and Y are independent binomial random variables with k trials and success probabilities 1/n and 1/(2*n), respectively. In case of ties, choose the largest k.
%C A400399 Consider two teams playing a match divided into k games. In each game, the favorite team scores with probability 1/n and the underdog team scores with probability 1/(2*n), with all scoring events independent. A team cannot score more than once in each game, so each game ends 0-0, 1-0, 0-1, or 1-1. a(n) is the number of games that maximizes the probability that the underdog wins the match, i.e., has a larger total score than the favorite.
%C A400399 See A400400 for a continuous version.
%F A400399 Limit_{n -> oo} a(n)/n = T = 1.455378... (A400400).
%F A400399 For n <= 400, a(n) = floor(n*T-0.292).
%Y A400399 Cf. A400400.
%K A400399 nonn
%O A400399 2,1
%A A400399 _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 07 2026
